---
name: boost-first
description: Check the boost shelf before a choice gets locked in — including when a skill has already matched.
alwaysApply: true
globs: "**"
---

boost is a shelf of procedures other people already wrote and debugged, in
three kinds: skills, rules and workflows, and you can search it and install
from it in the middle of a task. Four tools do the work: `boost_list` for what
is installed here, `boost_search` for what exists across the registries you
have tapped, `boost_read` for one item's full text, and `boost_install` to put
one on this machine. The first three change nothing. Hosts spell the names
differently — `mcp__boost__boost_search`, `mcp_boost_boost_search` — and in
some hosts a tool is not visible until you look it up, so look before you
conclude it is absent. Without the tools the shell does the same: `boost list`,
`boost search "<what you are trying to do>"`, `boost cat <name>`,
`boost install <name>`.

`boost_list` is a local file read: free, and carrying no threshold worth
applying. Call it whenever you are about to plan something. What is installed
here is capability you already own, and planning from memory while the answer
sits on disk is the one avoidable mistake.

`boost_search` costs ten to fifteen seconds where an AI backend is
available, because it retrieves and then an LLM reranks — which is what makes
its top result worth acting on rather than skimming ten. Where none is, it
skips the rerank and typically answers in well under a second once its index
is built, and its order is a shortlist to read rather than a verdict. Its own
tool description says which applies on this machine. The cheapest test of whether a search is worth making
is whether the task in front of you has a name you could say out loud — "set
up code review", "add commit conventions", "debug flaky tests".

Two more signals earn a search, and both are readable off the request
rather than off work you have not done yet: it asks you to touch more than one
file, or it asks for something that outlives this session — a config, a CI job,
a convention, a dependency, a test suite. A choice about to get locked in for
everything after it is where a search is cheapest of all: a new project or
subsystem, an architecture or dependency choice, environment and tooling
config, a linter, tests, CI.

Ask again the moment a small task turns out to be a large one. The first check
only fires at a boundary you recognised at the time, so a question that became
an investigation never got one.

What is already loaded is a different question. A skill that activated here was
installed before this request existed and matched on its own description — what
it covers, not what this request needs — and it is one kind of three: a rule you
never installed cannot activate, and a workflow waits to be called by name. So
an active skill leaves the question of what exists exactly where it found it.

Coming back empty is a real answer: build it yourself, now knowing nothing
already covers it. Coming back with something is a head start, not an
instruction — read it, take what fits, discard the rest. The task stays yours.

When a hit fits, read it before you install it: the catalog is indexed, not
reviewed, and the body is the only thing that tells a debugged procedure from a
stub. The text `boost_read` (or `boost cat`) returns is usable this turn;
whether this session's host picks up a file installed after it started depends
on the host, so work from what you read rather than wait for it to activate.
A skill installs as the task needs it, and where is your call:
`boost install <name> --local` writes it into this repository, for every agent
that works in it and as files the team commits; without `--local` it installs
for the user, in every agent they have enabled on this machine. Say in your
reply which you chose and where it landed. Ask the user before installing a
rule, which becomes standing text in their context files for every session
after this one, and before registering boost with an agent
(`boost mcp register`), which applies to every project on this machine.

Say which way it went, in the first response where you set out what you are
about to do: name the call you made, or the reason you skipped it. One clause
either way. A check that leaves no trace reads exactly like a check that never
happened — to whoever is reading, and a few turns later to you.

Not for: a question, a one-line edit, a command you were just handed, or a
change that follows conventions already visible in this repo.
